menu "Application config"

    config EXAMPLE_WIFI_SSID
        string "access point to join"
        default "your-ssid"
        ---help---
            Power save is negotiated with the access point, so the example has to be
            associated before there is anything to turn on.

    config EXAMPLE_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "access point password"
        default "your-password"

    config EXAMPLE_WIFI_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S
        int "seconds to wait for an address before giving up"
        default 30
        range 5 300

    config EXAMPLE_WIFI_PS
        bool "enable wifi power save"
        default y
        ---help---
            Turn this off to measure what power save is worth, and note that it does not
            mean "skip the call": platforms commonly switch power save on by themselves the
            moment the interface becomes a station, so the example has to ask for it to be
            off explicitly, or the comparison measures the same thing twice.

    config EXAMPLE_WIFI_PS_DTIM
        depends on EXAMPLE_WIFI_PS
        int "DTIM interval to listen on"
        default 1
        range 1 10
        ---help---
            How many beacon intervals the radio may sleep through between wakeups. The
            access point buffers traffic for a dozing station and delivers it on the DTIM
            beacon, so a larger value means fewer wakeups and lower current, at the cost of
            that much more latency for anything waiting.

            1 wakes on every DTIM the access point sends, which is the lowest latency and
            the highest current. Most access points send one every 1-3 beacons, and a
            beacon is typically 102.4 ms.

endmenu
